r international globalization but  that's not in the cards I start  available to the United States we have  neither the wealth nor the power to  force that and consequently it's a  painful adjustment in which you make  lots of noise about rewriting the rules  of international trade which you cannot  do in order to improve your national  situation which has been sacrificed  considerably for the money to be made by  globalization and again mr. Trump can  twist and turn and irate all he wants  these basic adjustments in which the  United States is ratcheting down  becoming less of an important player in  the world economy as India and China and  places like that move up into that  position capitalism doesn't stay still  and it doesn't stay still for United  States any more than it did for anybody  else before or that it will in the  future and this reality again is what is  not being discussed neither by the  bushes nor by the Clintons nor by mr.  Trump who wants to continue living in  the way we can fix all of this just give  me a little more time and I'll take care  of economist Richard Wolff professor  emeritus of economics at my alma mater  the University of Massachusetts in  Amherst  thanks as always for joining me thank  you David look forward to the next time
 

